**Question 2:** What is the difference between `__proto__` and `prototype`?

No problem — let me explain it simply.

## Prototypes (the 2-minute version)

Every object in JS has a hidden link called `__proto__` that points to another object — its **prototype**. If JS can't find a property on an object, it walks up that chain until it finds it or reaches `null`.

```js
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
arr.map(...); // arr doesn't have .map(), but its prototype does
```

**The chain:** `arr → Array.prototype → Object.prototype → null`

**Two ways to set up prototypes:**

| Constructor functions (old) | Classes (modern) |
|---|---|
| `function Dog(name) { this.name = name; }` | `class Dog { constructor(name) { this.name = name; } }` |
| `Dog.prototype.bark = function() { ... }` | `bark() { ... }` |
| `const d = new Dog("Rex");` | `const d = new Dog("Rex");` |

They do the same thing — classes are just cleaner syntax.

