### Why `.then` if we have `try/catch`?

You're right — with `async/await`, you almost never need `.then()`. Use `try/catch` instead.

**Cases where `.then` is still useful:**

```js
// 1. Fire-and-forget (no need to await)
fetch("/api/log").then(() => {});
// vs
await fetch("/api/log"); // pointless await

// 2. One-liner transformations
Promise.resolve(42).then(x => x * 2);

// 3. Libraries that return Promises (not async/await friendly)
someLibrary.doSomething().then(result => ...);
```

**Rule of thumb:** If you're writing async/await code, use `try/catch`. Only use `.then()` for fire-and-forget or simple chaining.

